# Senya 1st Landing — environment variables (example)
#
# Copy this file to `.env` (or `.env.local`) and fill in real values. The real file is gitignored
# (see .gitignore: `.env`, `.env.*`, except this `.env.example`). Never commit secrets.
#
# This is the onboarding contract: every credential the app needs has a documented slot here, with a
# placeholder value — so a new machine knows exactly what to provide. Keep it in sync with the code;
# add a line the moment a new secret is introduced (house standard: secrets are server-side only).

# ── App ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
NODE_ENV=development
# APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ── Data store (Google Sheets, via a service account — ADR-007) ──────
# Server-side only — read by the two API routes, never by client code. Set up once: create a GCP
# project, enable the Sheets API, create a service account, download its key, then share the target
# spreadsheet with the service account's email (Editor access). The sheet needs one tab named "Leads"
# with a header row: id, name, email, phone, locale, created_at.
GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_EMAIL=
# Paste the service account key's private_key value with literal \n for newlines (as it appears in
# the downloaded JSON), wrapped in quotes.
GOOGLE_PRIVATE_KEY=
GOOGLE_SHEETS_SPREADSHEET_ID=

# ── Backend / hosting (Vercel serverless functions — ADR-002) ────────
# The shared access code checked by POST /api/verify-code. Server-side only — never sent to the
# client in any form (ADR-004).
ACCESS_CODE=
